    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d227">

                <pb facs="0587" n="547" xml:id="pg547"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">674.87/8–2754: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 227<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in
                        Iraq</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d227-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-08-31T16:40:00-04:00">August 31, 1954—4:40
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>119. Cairos 6 to Baghdad<anchor n="2" xml:id="d227-f2"/> confirms our
                    understanding current Egyptian attitude against association at present with West
                    in area defense arrangements.</p>
                <p>In view Egyptian Government’s apparent rejection Nuri Said–Saleh Salem proposals
                    (which could not have been acceptable to USG in context present orientation
                    ALCSP) Embassy should at appropriate opportunity sound out Nuri on his present
                    thinking. Embassy may in its discretion mention Department’s satisfaction over
                    assurance given Ambassador Berry by Nuri re his support for Turk–Pakistan pact
                    (Embtel 590)<anchor n="3" xml:id="d227-f3"/> and Nuri’s statement (Embtel
                        105).<anchor n="4" xml:id="d227-f4"/> He not abandoning regional defense
                    principle which he recognized lay behind pact. Having in mind these statements
                    and last paragraph Embtel 107<anchor n="5" xml:id="d227-f5"/> we hope Nuri will
                    focus his attention on Turki-Pakistan pact which we believe offers only basis
                    for realistic and effective defense planning in Near East.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
